// 3.4 NEGATIVE test — a write through a read-only view must ABORT LOUDLY.
//
// This program is REQUIRED TO FAIL (non-zero exit). Run by _bytes_view_gate.ps1, which asserts
// the non-zero exit and the diagnostic text.
//
// Why a panic and not a silent no-op: a view aliases the parent's storage, so a write that "just
// works" would corrupt a buffer the writer does not own, and one that silently does nothing would
// lose data with no indication why. Both are the silent-wrong-data class this project treats as the
// worst outcome. Loud abort is the only honest option at runtime.
//
// Why not a compile error: enforcing read-only in the type system means a new capability in the
// type checker, which is a much larger blast radius. Tracked separately; the runtime guard is what
// makes the feature SAFE to ship now.
//
// bytes_append is the dangerous one and the reason this guard is mandatory, not cosmetic: on a view
// `size >= cap` holds by construction, so an unguarded append would call nova_back_grow on BORROWED
// storage and realloc/free the PARENT's buffer out from under it.

fn main()
    let src = bytes_create(8)
    bytes_set(src, 0, 11)
    let v = bytes_view(src, 0, 4)
    print("before write")
    bytes_set(v, 0, 99)
    print("UNREACHABLE: write through a read-only view was allowed")
